[Event "Nice"]
[Site "Nice FRA"]
[Date "1930.??.??"]
[White "Edgard Colle"]
[Black "John O'Hanlon"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "D05"]

{The most quoted Greek gift of the twentieth century. Colle reaches it from his own opening system, and Black chooses the Kg6 defence, so we get to see how the attack continues when the king walks out.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. e3 c5 4. c3 e6 5. Bd3 Bd6 6. Nbd2 Nbd7 7. O-O O-O 8. Re1 Re8 9. e4 {The Colle System's thematic break. The bishop on d3 now looks at h7.} dxe4 10. Nxe4 Nxe4 11. Bxe4 cxd4 {Black grabs a pawn and, with it, removes the knight from f6. Count the checklist: bishop aims at h7, knight can reach g5, queen can come to h5, no defender of h7.} 12. Bxh7+ Kxh7 13. Ng5+ Kg6 {Refusing to go back. On g8, Qh5 would threaten mate at once.} 14. h4 {The standard answer to Kg6: the h-pawn joins the hunt and threatens h5+.} Rh8 15. Rxe6+ {A second sacrifice to strip the king. Taking is impossible because of Qd3+ and Nxf7.} Nf6 16. h5+ Kh6 17. Rxd6 Qa5 18. Nxf7+ Kh7 19. Ng5+ Kg8 20. Qb3+ {Mate follows on the light squares.} 1-0
